course: dictionary
lesson: 10
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words wear more than one coat. Abide is one of them, and the words around it show you which meaning to use. «I can't abide cold tea.» The clue is cold tea, so abide means you dislike it so much you cannot put up with it.
explain: «Pupils must abide by the rules.» The words by the rules are the clue. Abide by means to obey or follow. Swap in obey, and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: In old stories, abide means stay or live somewhere. The hymn «Abide with Me» uses it this way, as an old-fashioned word for remain. When you meet it in old text, try stay first.
explain: The Duchess in «Alice in Wonderland» says, «I never could abide figures!» Figures means numbers, so abide means stand, as in can't stand. The clue is the word figures, which tells you what she cannot stand.
fact: Abide has three main meanings: dislike, obey, and stay.
fact: «Can't abide» means you dislike something so much you cannot put up with it.
fact: «Abide by» means to obey or follow a rule, law or decision, as in «law-abiding».
fact: Old-fashioned abide means to live, stay or remain somewhere.
fact: «I never could abide figures!» means «I never could stand numbers!»
exercise: «Pupils must abide by the rules.» What does abide by mean here? | Feel sad about | *Obey or follow | Break or ignore | Make up new ones
exercise: «I can't abide cold tea.» What does can't abide mean? | Drink it quickly | Love it very much | *Dislike it so much you cannot put up with it | Heat it up
exercise: In «I never could abide figures!», which word could abide be swapped for? | Count | Forget | Draw | *Stand
test: In an old story, a knight says, «We shall abide here until the storm passes.» What does abide mean? | *Stay | Sing | Sleep at the top of the hill | Fight
test: Which sentence uses abide to mean obey? | The hymn was sung at the cup final. | *Everyone must abide by the school's safety rules. | I can't abide noisy dogs. | The Duchess had a sharp chopper.
exam: Mia says abide always means dislike. Is she right? | Yes, it always means dislike | Only in «Alice in Wonderland» | *No, it can also mean obey, or stay | Only when it is sung in a hymn
